{
  "id": "COMPOUND:14268 - PROTEIN:4738",
  "verbalization": "Benzocaine is implicated in the target pathway through multiple indirect links, all of which are mediated by 4-(3-Pyridin-2-Yl-1h-Pyrazol-4-Yl)Quinoline. This compound acts as a common target of benzocaine, which in turn influences the activity of Serine/threonine-protein kinase TBK1, Cathepsin S, and Ephrin type-B receptor 6. Each of these targets ultimately leads to the activation of the Phosphatidylinositol 4-phosphate 3-kinase C2 domain-containing subunit beta. The shared involvement of 4-(3-Pyridin-2-Yl-1h-Pyrazol-4-Yl)Quinoline as a common mediator across these pathways suggests a potential mechanism where benzocaine modulates the activity of these receptors, which in turn activate the kinase. This interconnectedness highlights a possible role for benzocaine in regulating intracellular signaling pathways involving phosphatidylinositol 4-phosphate, which is critical for cellular processes such as vesicle trafficking and membrane dynamics. The evidence supports a mechanism where benzocaine exerts its effects through a common intermediary, 4-(3-Pyridin-2-Yl-1h-Pyrazol-4-Yl)Quinoline, which then influences downstream targets in a coordinated manner.",
